<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1918 CE · capital Zagreb</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Brief state of the South Slavs of Austria-Hungary, proclaimed in Zagreb in October 1918 and merged into Yugoslavia a month later.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> disintegrated, the National Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>, formed earlier in October 1918, took power. On 29 October the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-parliament">Croatian Sabor</a> broke ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and the new state was proclaimed, covering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-slavonia">Croatia-Slavonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>, with the Slovene politician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-korosec">Anton Korošec</a> as president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The state had no army and no international recognition, and its authority was weak in the countryside, where returning soldiers and peasants rioted. The Austro-Hungarian fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pula">Pola</a> was handed to it, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> seized <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/histria">Istria</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svetozar-pribicevic">Svetozar Pribićević</a> led those pushing for rapid union with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Fearing Italian expansion, the National Council sent a delegation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, and on 1 December 1918 the state united with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia</a> to form the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes</a>, later called the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs.jpg" alt="State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Orlovic: I drew this flag in photoshop, on the base of another version on the internet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_State_of_Slovenes,_Croats_and_Serbs.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zagreb</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Anton Korošec, Svetozar Pribićević</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic and Orthodox Christianity, Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Croatian, Slovene, Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a href="/countries/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Država Slovenaca, Hrvata i Srba</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs" aria-label="State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5058 563 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5378 819L5378 819M5378 819L5365 872M5378 819L5442 880M5378 819L5380 747M5378 819L5488 850"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5378 819h0M5365 872h0M5442 880h0M5380 747h0M5488 850h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5378 819h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5378 819h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Zagreb. <a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
